		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1984#p1984"><![CDATA[
You can adjust the center of mass by using compound shapes: just translate the collision shape. I will make some demo to demonstrate this.<br>Bullet uses a full inertial tensor, but it assumes that the main axis of inertia is aligned along the coordinate axis. In that case you can store the inertia as main diagonal (vector3).<br>The solver uses a 3x3 matrix, the inverse inertia tensor.<br><br>I might add an explicit COM shift routine, but right now the is a workaround by shifting the collision model around the 'center of mass'. It gives the same effect.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Sat Sep 30, 2006 3:07 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
